the magnitude of devastation is hard to  take in  but shows the power of storm eunice  a 400 year old tree ripped from its root  destroying this family home in essex in  seconds i was in a conference call in  that room sven good was working  downstairs when the tree hit i heard  this gigantic creek and then a huge bang  and the whole house shook and i sort of  jumped i'm sorry guys i've got to get  off  i ran to see if my girlfriend was okay  who she didn't even know that the house  had gone down  and then me and my mom both ran upstairs  and checked out my room which now has no  wall went to see my sister's room and  that's just a scene of chaos really this  is the view inside that bedroom it will  be a long wait to fully assess the  damage but it could have been a lot  worse this was a very lucky escape for  this family who've lived here in this  house for generations and like many up  and down the country they now face a  huge cleanup operation and are hoping  that the weather doesn't hamper their  efforts  work will soon begin in southampton to  return this roof to its rightful owner  a block of flats down the road  on this london street the strong winds  turned a roof into rubble and a  children's playground into a danger zone  the iconic o2 arena will remain closed  so staff can make it safe after winds  tore through its roof  there's also been a second day of severe  travel disruption with snow grinding  roads to a halt in the north and trains  cancelled as trees and debris are taken  off the tracks  trains are in the wrong locations  there's still  debris being removed from our roads  airports are expected to be very busy  people catching up with flights  and potentially queues of ports  a busy and confusing time for some  people waiting to get home our train was  booked at 10 o'clock but it's been  delayed and we've  we've just heard there might be one  going in a minute  fingers crossed i managed to get my  ticket refunded but in terms of actually  getting home i'm not really sure and  then i turned up a paddington to get my  connection to the bath and completely  closed no trains at all  so i stayed the night with my family in  london  came back and now bart's not even on the  thing so i'm going to ask them now  storm eunice was one of the worst to hit  the uk in decades it's going to take  time to clear the widespread destruction  left in its wake charlotte lomas sky  news
 

